<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tlacaelel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1397">1397</a>–<a href="/years/1487">1487</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tlacaelel was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> statesman and adviser to several rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in the fifteenth century. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> describe him as the ideologist who rewrote Mexica history to provide the theological and political foundation of empire. He recast the Mexica as a chosen people with a sacred mission, closely tied to the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tlacaelel.jpg" alt="Tlacaelel" width="225" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tlacochcalcatl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tlacaelel, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> name means Man of Strong Emotions, was the son of the ruler Huitzilihuitl and Queen Cacamacihuatl. He was a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a>, a brother of the rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimalpopoca">Chimalpopoca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a>, and the father of the poet Macuilxochitzin. He is regarded as the principal architect of the Aztec Triple Alliance and so of the Mexica empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a> he held the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacochcalcatl">Tlacochcalcatl</a>, and during the war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepanecs">Tepanecs</a> in the late 1420s he was raised to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cihuacoatl">Cihuacoatl</a>, first adviser to the ruler. He kept that office under four successive rulers until his death. He raised the tribal god and hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a> to the head of the pantheon and increased the militarism of the state. According to the chronicler Diego Durán, he increased <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>, above all during a period of natural disasters that began in 1446, and the flower wars against Tlaxcala and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> city-states were his invention in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a>.</p><p>To strengthen the nobility he helped create and enforce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumptuary-law">sumptuary</a> laws that forbade commoners to wear lip plugs, gold armbands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloaks. He is also credited with a policy of burning the books of conquered peoples, so as to erase the memory of a past before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1484 Tlacaelel dedicated the seventh reconstruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> with the sacrifice of many war captives, at a time when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> power stood at its height. After his death in 1487 the empire went on expanding north into the Gran Chichimeca and south towards the Maya lands. The Mexican writer Antonio Velasco Piña made him the hero of the novel Tlacaelel, El Azteca entre los Aztecas in 1979, and Amado Zumaya played him in Juan Mora Catlett's 1990 film Return to Aztlán.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tlacaelel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the same time the statesman <b>Tlacaelel</b> reshaped Mexica history to supply a theological foundation for empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1455" aria-label="Tenochtitlan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tlacaelel"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4267 5869h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4267 5869h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.44,-99.13&amp;year=1455">What was here in 1455? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
